From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 19:34:00 +0200
Subject: MIPS: mm: bound the uncached alias to the KSEG1 DRAM window

arch_dma_set_uncached() computes an uncached alias for every physical
address it is handed, and on intel-mips (Lantiq/Intel xRX500) not every
physical address has one.

The SegCtl this platform boots under maps kernel VA 0xA0000000-0xBFFFFFFF
uncached onto physical 0x20000000-0x3FFFFFFF, and only the lower half of
that is DRAM. The upper half -- VA 0xB0000000 and up -- is the SoC's
register space, which appears there at its nominal address plus
0x20000000, and which every ioremap() on this platform is served out of.
So the uncached alias of DRAM is exactly the first 256 MiB, and
PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M is where it stops being one.

The vendor kernel states the same window three ways. Its plat_ioremap()
maps [PHYS_OFFSET, PHYS_OFFSET + GRX500_UNCACHED_RAM_IOREMAP_SIZE)
through CKSEG1 and the register range 0x10000000-0x1FFFFFFF through a
flat GRX500_REG_IOREMAP_PHYS_VIRT_OFFSET of 0xA0000000, that size
constant being 0x10000000 (vendor GPL release of Linux 4.9,
arch/mips/include/asm/mach-lantiq/ioremap.h). It caps its CMA region at
PHYS_OFFSET + min(ZONE_DMA_SIZE, SZ_256M) because "the system has only
256MB uncached address available" (arch/mips/kernel/setup.c). And the
window size is a constant in every release; only the ZONE_DMA sub-window
carved out of it is configurable.

Above the line the alias is a register address, and what happens then
depends on whether anything is decoded there. On an unpopulated hole it
is a data bus error: a coherent page at physical 0x31477000 aliases onto
nominal 0x11477000, nothing answers, and a kernel-mode memcpy through it
panicked this silicon. On a populated block there is no fault at all --
the write lands in the register while the device goes on reading the
untouched DRAM page, and the damage surfaces in a block with no
connection to the driver that allocated.

Nothing else on this platform bounds where a coherent allocation lands.
The DMA mask does not: xhci-plat and dwc3 both ask for DMA_BIT_MASK(64),
CONFIG_ARCH_DMA_ADDR_T_64BIT is unset so it truncates to 0xffffffff, and
dma_coherent_ok() then accepts every physical address in the machine.
The zones do not: there is no ZONE_DMA32 on a 32-bit kernel, MIPS never
assigns zone_dma_limit so it stays below PHYS_OFFSET and
dma_direct_optimal_gfp_mask() never picks GFP_DMA, and ZONE_NORMAL runs
from 0x21000000 to the top of RAM. CONFIG_CMA is not set and no DT node
carries dma-coherent or a restricted-dma-pool. On a 512 MiB board that
leaves roughly 256 MiB freely allocatable and unusable, with the
placement decided by the buddy allocator.

Boards at 256 MiB cannot reach the line and are unaffected. On the one
that can, the exposure is not first probe -- early allocations come off
the low end of the free lists, because memblock frees ascending and
__free_pages_core() adds with FPI_TO_TAIL -- but runtime growth: a
dma_pool page added on hotplug, a ring expansion, a stream context
array. That is the shape of a latent, non-reproducible bug, which is the
argument for catching it here rather than triaging it later.

So bound it and fail closed. dma_direct_alloc() already handles an
ERR_PTR from this function: it frees the pages and returns NULL, and the
driver sees an ordinary allocation failure. A visible -ENOMEM under
memory pressure on one board is a better trade than a silent write into
SoC registers, and the check costs a compare on a path that already does
a virt-to-phys.

Failing closed silently would not be, though. dma_direct_alloc() drops
the errno, so what the log gets is the driver's own report of an
allocation that returned nothing, and on a board with hundreds of
megabytes free that reads as a leak somewhere else:

  ath10k_pci 0000:01:00.0: failed to alloc htt rx: -12

The rate-limited line therefore names the allocation and the bound it
crossed, so a burst of refusals is visible as a burst; and the one-shot
WARN is there for the backtrace, because this function is handed an
address and a length and no caller identity. Every coherent allocation
on the platform arrives through dma_direct_alloc(), so a return address
names that and nothing else -- the stack is the only thing that says
which driver asked.

This function is the single point every dma_alloc_coherent() on the
platform passes through.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/mm/dma-noncoherent.c b/arch/mips/mm/dma-noncoherent.c
index 96400a9fd294..e89b07a1e160 100644
--- a/arch/mips/mm/dma-noncoherent.c
+++ b/arch/mips/mm/dma-noncoherent.c
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 #include <linux/dma-direct.h>
 #include <linux/dma-map-ops.h>
 #include <linux/highmem.h>
+#include <linux/sizes.h>
 
 #include <asm/cache.h>
 #include <asm/cpu-type.h>
@@ -49,8 +50,38 @@ void arch_dma_prep_coherent(struct page *page, size_t size)
 	dma_cache_wback_inv((unsigned long)page_address(page), size);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Upper bound on the physical addresses an uncached alias may be taken of.
+ * Above it the uncached window maps registers rather than DRAM, so a coherent
+ * allocation placed there would be handed a register address.
+ */
+#define INTEL_MIPS_UNCAC_DRAM_LIMIT	(PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M)
+
 void *arch_dma_set_uncached(void *addr, size_t size)
 {
+	unsigned long pa = __pa(addr);
+
+	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_INTEL_MIPS) &&
+	    pa + size > INTEL_MIPS_UNCAC_DRAM_LIMIT) {
+		/*
+		 * Say so. dma_direct_alloc() discards this errno and hands
+		 * the caller a plain NULL, so a refusal reads as ordinary
+		 * memory pressure -- on a board with hundreds of MiB free,
+		 * and only once the free lists have drifted above the bound,
+		 * which is why it does not reproduce on a freshly booted
+		 * machine.
+		 *
+		 * The rate-limited line identifies every refusal; the
+		 * one-shot backtrace identifies the caller, which nothing in
+		 * this frame can.
+		 */
+		pr_err_ratelimited("uncached alias refused: pa 0x%lx size %zu, KSEG1 limit 0x%lx\n",
+				   pa, size, INTEL_MIPS_UNCAC_DRAM_LIMIT);
+		WARN_ONCE(1, "coherent DMA allocation above the KSEG1 DRAM window\n");
+
+		return ERR_PTR(-ENOMEM);
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * __pa() yields an address measured from physical zero on the legacy
 	 * memory map, which is also where the uncached window at UNCAC_BASE
@@ -59,9 +90,9 @@ void *arch_dma_set_uncached(void *addr, size_t size)
 	 * of DRAM, so the anchor has to be taken back out.
 	 */
 	if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_EVA))
-		return (void *)(__pa(addr) - PHYS_OFFSET + UNCAC_BASE);
+		return (void *)(pa - PHYS_OFFSET + UNCAC_BASE);
 
-	return (void *)(__pa(addr) + UNCAC_BASE);
+	return (void *)(pa + UNCAC_BASE);
 }
 
 static inline void dma_sync_virt_for_device(void *addr, size_t size,
